1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Fundamentals of Pathology, Chapter 2.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,000
This chapter deals with inflammation, inflammatory disorders, and wound healing.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
As a brief introduction before we even begin the discussion,

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
we should remind ourselves what inflammation actually is.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
And maybe the easiest way to do this would be to make a simple diagram,

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,000
so I'm going to create a little space here for that.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Imagine that this is a blood vessel,

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
and we're well aware of the fact that within blood vessels

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
is flowing inflammatory cells, for example, neutrophils.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Also present within blood vessels are lymphocytes.

11
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
And within blood vessels is fluid and other important proteins.

12
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Now, essentially the goal of inflammation is to take what is in the blood vessel

13
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,000
and to put it out here into the tissue space, usually in response to an infection.

14
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,000
If the neutrophils are the primary cells that come out of the blood vessel and into the tissue space,

15
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,000
we call that acute inflammation.

16
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
And if the lymphocytes are the primary cells that come out into the tissue space,

17
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
then we call that chronic inflammation.

18
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
And that's a very, very simplistic way of looking at inflammation,

19
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,000
but I do think that that's sort of the global picture that we have to have in mind before we move forward.

20
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
So let's go back and review what's in your text.

21
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:33,000
So we say that inflammation allows inflammatory cells, plasma proteins, and fluid to exit the blood vessel

22
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
and enter the interstitial space.

23
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,000
And remember that the interstitial space is basically the tissue space outside of the blood vessel.

24
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
It's where the inflammation is actually occurring.

25
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,000
And this can be divided into acute inflammation and chronic inflammation.

26
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:55,000
And again, acute inflammation will be hallmarked by neutrophils and chronic inflammation by lymphocytes.

27
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:02,000
So your book, your text, then goes into acute inflammation, and that's going to be the next major subtopic.

28
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
So acute inflammation is characterized by two things.

29
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Number one, edema, and number two, neutrophils.

30
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Now by edema, what we're referring to is fluid that comes from the blood vessels

31
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
and begins to accumulate within the tissue space.

32
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:23,000
And that's what causes the swelling that we normally see when there is inflammation.

33
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
And then neutrophils refers to the key inflammatory cell that comes out of the connective tissue space,

34
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
sorry, out of the blood vessel into the connective tissue space that then defines acute inflammation.

35
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Here's a picture of acute inflammation, and it highlights a little bit of what we've discussed.

36
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
First of all, notice that we are outside of the blood vessel.

37
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Here's a blood vessel, and here's a blood vessel.

38
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,000
And the vast majority of what you see on your screen here is actually the tissue space.

39
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
And so we notice that within the tissue space are these multilobated cells,

40
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
which actually are neutrophils, all of these cells that I'm circling here.

41
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:08,000
And each of these cells, the fact that there are neutrophils present, indicates that we're dealing with acute inflammation.

42
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Now at the same time, notice that there is this separation by this sort of white fluid of the tissue space,

43
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
and all of this separation represents edema, or the fluid that's actually building up within the tissue space.

44
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:33,000
And so again, the hallmarks of acute inflammation are neutrophils along with edema.

45
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:40,000
So the next important point to be aware of is that acute inflammation arises in response to two important stimuli.

46
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Again, two important stimuli.

47
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,000
The first is infection, and I think we're all well aware of the fact that when there's infection,

48
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:55,000
particularly bacterial infection, one of the key responses is going to be the neutrophilic response,

49
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
which will then come in and create, which will then come in and destroy the organisms.

50
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
So I don't think that is so important at this particular junction, because we're familiar with it.

51
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:13,000
But what perhaps is a little more important is the fact that tissue necrosis is something that leads to acute inflammation.

52
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
And this is the second major stimulus of acute inflammation.

53
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Now go back and remind ourselves from Chapter 1 that there are two types of cell death.

54
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
The first is necrosis, and the second is apoptosis.

55
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:34,000
And one of the key distinguishing features between necrosis and apoptosis is that necrosis is followed by acute inflammation.

56
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:41,000
This is a very important sort of high yield point, so that whenever there is tissue necrosis,

57
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:48,000
one of the responses of the body to that necrosis will be to send neutrophils in to destroy the tissue.

58
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:58,000
And once the tissue is destroyed, it will then kick off the healing and repair phases of sort of wound healing.

59
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:07,000
And so basically this is important because tissue necrosis is another mediator of acute inflammation.

60
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Now some classic examples that we're going to touch on later that you should be familiar with.

61
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,000
One of the things that we're going to discuss when we talk about myocardial infarction is a response to the body.

62
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
What happens 12 hours after an infarction?

63
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
What happens pathologically 24 hours after an infarction?

64
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
And at that point we're going to learn that 24 hours after an infarction,

65
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
one of the key hallmarks on the slide is going to be the presence of acute inflammation in neutrophils.

66
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Another important point clinically here would be that let's say a patient has a myocardial infarction.

67
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
One of the things that's going to occur with that myocardial infarction will be an increase in the patient's white count.

68
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
And what the white count will predominantly contain are neutrophils because neutrophils are being generated

69
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:57,000
and they are being pushed off, will then be subsequently pushed off into the dead tissue.

70
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:03,000
So very important to know that tissue necrosis is also a cause of acute inflammation.

71
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:08,000
What's the goal in each of these circumstances?

72
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Well, the goal in the case of infection would be to eliminate the pathogen.

73
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
So the neutrophils come in, they consume.

74
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
That's the way by which neutrophils kill.

75
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
They eat and then they destroy that which they eat.

76
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
So they consume and then destroy the pathogen.

77
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,000
And in the case of tissue necrosis, the goal would be to clear the necrotic debris.

78
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
That's the purpose of the acute inflammation.

79
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
The neutrophils come in and then they consume the dead or dying tissue

80
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:41,000
and then they destroy that tissue so that it can be subsequently cleared so that healing can actually begin.

81
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:47,000
One of the other important features of acute inflammation is the fact that it is an immediate response.

82
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
It is a very quick response of the body.

83
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:51,000
It happens.

84
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Basically, the acute inflammation, once it's kicked off, very quickly fluid begins to build up within the tissue.

85
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:03,000
And within 24 hours, the neutrophils have pretty much arrived and are sort of at the peak of their function.

86
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,000
And so it's a very, very quick response.

87
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
It's an immediate response.

88
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:13,000
However, at the expense of that quickness is specificity so that it has a limited specificity.

89
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Now, what I mean by limited specificity is that it's not like the chronic inflammatory response

90
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:25,000
where you have an antibody or a T cell receptor against a very specific antigen.

91
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:30,000
In this particular case, it's sort of a generalized response against the stimulus.

92
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:34,000
You don't have a very specific attack of a particular antigen.

93
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:40,000
And it is an example, or basically it is one component of innate immunity.

94
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,000
So this requires us to take a step back to understand innate immunity.

95
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:45,000
What is innate immunity?

96
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
The innate immunity basically includes the following.

97
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
It includes the epithelium that covers body surfaces.

98
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,000
The epithelium protects against infection.

99
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:00,000
It includes the mucus that's secreted by cells because the mucus layer that's produced by those cells

100
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
protects against infection.

101
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
It includes the complement system.

102
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,000
The complement system is a series of proteins that are present in an inactive state within the serum

103
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,000
but can be activated to generate an inflammatory response.

104
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:18,000
And it includes some cells, for example, mass cells, which are widely distributed throughout tissue

105
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
and can help to activate the immune response.

106
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:32,000
Macrophages, which have the ability to consume pathogens and then present them to further the immune response.

107
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,000
Neutrophils, as we're going to discuss in significant detail what the neutrophils do,

108
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:40,000
but they're part of innate immunity, eosinophils, basophils, et cetera.

109
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:49,000
So the innate immune system is a broad system that's nonspecific that defends the host against microbes.

110
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:56,000
And another way to understand the innate immunity would be to contrast it against adaptive immunity.

111
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:57,000
What is adaptive immunity?

112
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:03,000
It is a specific response, but it takes longer to generate, so it's not as quick.

113
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:12,000
And it includes things like, in particular, the lymphocytes, which produce antibodies and have T-cell receptors

114
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
that are against a very specific target.

115
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
So that's innate immunity.

116
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:23,000
The immune system, in particular here, acute inflammation, is mediated by several factors.

117
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:27,000
And examiners like to ask questions about these factors.

118
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:31,000
And these factors sort of produce a symphony called acute inflammation.

119
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,000
And so it is important to be aware of the individual factors.

120
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
And we're going to take a little bit of time to go through each of these.

121
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:42,000
So the first are toll-like receptors, also called TLRs.

122
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:47,000
And toll-like receptors are actually present on cells of the innate immune system,

123
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
for example, macrophages and dendritic cells.

124
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:58,000
And the toll-like receptors, they have the ability to recognize something called PAMPs,

125
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:03,000
PAMPs, Pathogen-Associated Molecular Patterns.

126
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:10,000
These are patterns of molecules that are present on pathogens, and they're commonly present on pathogens.

127
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:14,000
And the toll-like receptors, they recognize these general patterns.

128
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:22,000
And when they see this pattern, that then lets the body know that there is an invader present within the body.

129
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:27,000
And that turns on the acute inflammatory response in this particular case.

130
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:35,000
The classic example, or one of the very high-yield examples, I should say, is something called CD14.

131
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:40,000
CD14 is a toll-like receptor, and it is present on the surface of macrophages.

132
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:44,000
And it has the ability to recognize lipopolysaccharide, or LPS.

133
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:50,000
Now recall, which is actually high-yield, that LPS is on the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria.

134
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
So all gram-negative bacteria have LPS on their outer membrane.

135
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
So LPS is sort of like a PAMP.

136
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:02,000
It's a pathogen-associated molecular pattern, and it is recognized by CD14.

137
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:07,000
When CD14 sees that PAMP, it then activates the immune system.

138
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,000
And this, again, is very high-yield.

139
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:18,000
You do have to know that CD14 is present on macrophages, and that it recognizes this PAMP, which is lipopolysaccharide.

140
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:24,000
Once the toll-like receptor binds its PAMP, that results in activation.

141
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:34,000
And a signal is sent to the nucleus, and one of the key proteins, it's actually a transcription factor that's upregulated, is called NF-kappaB.

142
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Very high-yield.

143
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:41,000
NF-kappaB is, we can say, sort of the molecular switch.

144
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:42,000
It's the on switch.

145
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:48,000
So the toll-like receptor gets activated, which then results in upregulation of NF-kappaB.

146
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,000
And NF-kappaB is a molecular switch which turns on the acute inflammatory response.

147
00:11:52,000 --> 00:12:01,000
Once NF-kappaB is generated, that activates multiple immune response genes, which then leads to the production of multiple immune mediators.

148
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:04,000
So this becomes very high-yield.

149
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:11,000
Toll-like receptors are also present on cells of adaptive immunity, meaning they're also present on the lymphocytes,

150
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:16,000
and therefore they play an important role in mediating chronic inflammation.

151
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
The next of the mediators is arachidonic acid.

152
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:26,000
Arachidonic acid is actually released from the phospholipid cell membrane by phospholipase A2.

153
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:32,000
And once arachidonic acid is released, it can be acted on by one of two pathways.

154
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:38,000
There is a cyclooxygenase pathway, and there is a 5-lipooxygenase pathway.

155
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:44,000
Each of these are distinct pathways, and they produce distinct, they generate distinct products.

156
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:49,000
So the cyclooxygenase pathway produces prostaglandin, PG, prostaglandin.

157
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
That's the key derivative of this pathway.

158
00:12:52,000 --> 00:13:00,000
And the prostaglandins, the three key prostaglandins are prostaglandin I, I2, D2, and then prostaglandin E2.

159
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:11,000
And together, these three, prostaglandin I2, D2, and E2, they mediate vasodilation, and they mediate increased vascular permeability.

160
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:16,000
Now vasodilation, it's important to know that the vasodilation occurs at the level of the arterial.

161
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:22,000
Generally speaking, wherever you're going to hear me talk about vasodilation in this particular discussion,

162
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:27,000
it occurs at the level of the arterial, and they increase vascular permeability.

163
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:34,000
And the increased vascular permeability, generally speaking, will occur at the post-capillary venule.

164
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:39,000
It's important to know both of these, i.e., that vasodilation occurs at the arterial,

165
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:45,000
and that increased vascular permeability occurs at the post-capillary venule.

166
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:51,000
In addition, prostaglandin E2 also mediates fever and pain.

167
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:56,000
And the way I remember that is, sort of fever is mediated by E2.

168
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,000
So prostaglandin E2 mediates fever.

169
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:05,000
So anyway, whatever helps you to remember it, but you do need to know that prostaglandin E2 mediates fever,

170
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
and it also mediates pain.

171
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,000
The lipo-oxygenase pathway produces leukotrienes.

172
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:20,000
And leukotriene, it produces in particular leukotriene B4, and then C4, D4, and E4.

173
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:26,000
So leukotriene B4, the key feature of that is that it attracts and activates neutrophils.

174
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
Very important.

175
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:31,000
Leukotriene B4 attracts and activates neutrophils.

176
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:37,000
Okay, I'm going to take a step back here, and now remind you that there are three key mediators.

177
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
Some people might say four.

178
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:45,000
So let's say four key mediators that actually attract and activate neutrophils.

179
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
The first is leukotriene B4.

180
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
The second is C5A.

181
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
The third is IL8.

182
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
And the fourth are bacterial products.

183
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:59,000
So these four mediators, these are the four that you need to sort of store away in the back of your mind.

184
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:06,000
Another group of leukotrienes that are important are leukotriene C4, D4, and E4.

185
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:12,000
Now leukotriene C4, D4, and E4, what they do is they mediate vasoconstriction,

186
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:17,000
they cause bronchospasm, and they increase vascular permeability.

187
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:18,000
So three actions.

188
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:24,000
Vasoconstriction, bronchospasm, and increased vascular permeability.

189
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:32,000
Leukotriene C4, leukotriene D4, and leukotriene E4, basically what they do is they contract smooth muscle.

190
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
They cause smooth muscle to contract.

191
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:42,000
So the arteriole is lined by smooth muscle, and when that arteriole contracts, you get smooth muscle contracts,

192
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
and we get vasoconstriction.

193
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,000
The bronchus is lined by smooth muscle, or contains smooth muscle on its wall,

194
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:53,000
and so when that smooth muscle contracts, we're going to get bronchospasm.

195
00:15:53,000 --> 00:16:01,000
An increased vascular permeability occurs when the pericytes, which contain smooth muscle contractile function,

196
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:08,000
when the pericytes contract increasing the opening or the space between the endothelial cells.

197
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:15,000
That might require a little bit of an explanation, so let me go back and remind you a little bit of normal histology.

198
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:19,000
Let's come here, and this is basically, I'm going to draw you a blood vessel.

199
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:25,000
Let's pretend that we're in the post-capillary venule, or in the capillary bed.

200
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:32,000
And so this is a blood vessel, and you recall from your histology that the blood vessel is lined by endothelial cells,

201
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:37,000
and the endothelial cells look like that, and that underneath the endothelial cells, on occasion,

202
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:43,000
is a second cell, which sits like this, sort of above the basement membrane, which is called a pericyte.

203
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:49,000
Now, the pericyte contains contractile function, and when this pericyte contracts,

204
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:58,000
it will separate or pull apart the endothelial cells so that, let's pretend that these are two endothelial cells,

205
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:03,000
so that when this contracts, it will open up the space between these two endothelial cells,

206
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:11,000
and that sort of then opening of the space allows for fluid to leak from the post-capillary venule out into the tissue space.

207
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:20,000
And so, again, when we think about, as I've said, when we think about these leukotrienes,

208
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:26,000
leukotriene C4, D4, let's erase that, leukotriene C4, D4, and E4,

209
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:32,000
they're going to mediate vasoconstriction by causing constriction of the arteriole,

210
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
the smooth muscle of the arteriole.

211
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:40,000
They're going to mediate bronchospasm by causing constriction of the smooth muscle wall of the bronchus,

212
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:46,000
and then increased vascular permeability by causing smooth muscle contraction of the pericytes,

213
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,000
which, again, contain contractile function.

214
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:52,000
So that's the way I remember leukotrienes.

215
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:56,000
The next of the mediators of acute inflammation are mast cells,

216
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:01,000
and mast cells are widely distributed throughout the connective tissue of the body.

217
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:06,000
There are three mechanisms by which mast cells can be activated.

218
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
Number one, tissue trauma.

219
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:11,000
The first way by which mast cells are activated is tissue trauma.

220
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:14,000
Whenever tissue trauma occurs, the mast cells get activated,

221
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:18,000
and they will then help to mediate the acute inflammatory response.

222
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:24,000
The second way by which mast cells can be activated are by the complement proteins C3A and C5A.

223
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:29,000
C3A and C5A are byproducts of the activation of the complement system.

224
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:32,000
We'll talk about them in more detail in a few minutes.

225
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:38,000
However, it's important to bear in mind here that these two proteins, C3A and C5A, they activate mast cells.

226
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:44,000
And the third way by which mast cells can be activated, and perhaps the one that we're most familiar with,

227
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:47,000
is cross-linking of surface IgE by antigen.

228
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:50,000
Cross-linking of surface IgE by antigen.

229
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:57,000
So the idea here is that you've got a mast cell, and we know that the mast cells express IgE on their surface.

230
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:01,000
And so here's one IgE molecule, and here's another IgE molecule.

231
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:08,000
And if an antigen comes by and cross-links these two IgE molecules, that then activates the mast cell.

232
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:12,000
So important to remember that there are three ways by which the mast cell is activated.

233
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
Number one, tissue trauma.

234
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:18,000
Number two, complement proteins C3A and C5A.

235
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:24,000
And number three, cross-linking of cell surface IgE by antigen.

236
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:28,000
Now once the mast cell gets activated, it undergoes an immediate response.

237
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:35,000
And the immediate response results in the dumping of histamine granules.

238
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:40,000
The immediate response results in the dumping of preformed histamine granules.

239
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:43,000
These histamine granules, they mediate two things.

240
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:46,000
Number one, the histamine granules cause vasodilation.

241
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
How will vasodilation occur?

242
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:49,000
You've already understood this.

243
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:54,000
It will occur by dilating the smooth muscle of the arterioles.

244
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:59,000
So the smooth muscle of the arterioles will dilate, which will then result in vasodilation.

245
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:04,000
At the same time, the histamines also have the ability to increase vascular permeability.

246
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
Where will increased vascular permeability occur?

247
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:10,000
The post-capillary venule.

248
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:13,000
So histamine has two primary functions.

249
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:22,000
Vasodilation at the level of the arterial and increased vascular permeability at the level of the post-capillary venule.

250
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
That's the immediate response.

251
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:30,000
That sort of occurs right when the mast cell gets activated.

252
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:37,000
The mast cell, once it dumps its preformed histamine granules, will also have a delayed or a late response,

253
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:42,000
in which it produces arachidonic acid metabolites, particularly leukotrienes.

254
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:47,000
And these leukotrienes allow for maintenance of the acute inflammatory response.

255
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:51,000
Very important because it's a common question that can show up on examinations.

256
00:20:51,000 --> 00:21:00,000
I could ask you, for example, that a patient has mast cells which activate the acute inflammatory response.

257
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:05,000
And several hours later, the acute inflammatory response continues.

258
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:13,000
What is the major mechanism by which mast cells will allow for the progression of the acute inflammatory response?

259
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:19,000
And the answer would be the production of arachidonic acids, particularly leukotrienes, high yield.

260
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:25,000
Leukotrienes are sort of the second phase of the mast cell response.

261
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:33,000
The next group of proteins that are important in acute inflammation is complement.

262
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:38,000
This is a group of pro-inflammatory serum proteins that complement inflammation.

263
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:39,000
They assist inflammation.

264
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
They help inflammation.

265
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:46,000
We're all familiar with the fact that complement circulates as inactive precursors,

266
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:53,000
and these precursors must be activated for the complement system to then cause its, to produce its effect.

267
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:58,000
The activation of the complement system occurs in three, by three particular pathways.

268
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
The first is the classical pathway.

269
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:05,000
In the classical pathway, C1, which is an early complement protein,

270
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:10,000
binds to IgG or IgM, which is bound to an antigen.

271
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:13,000
So IgG or IgM gets bound to an antigen.

272
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:19,000
C1 can bind to that bound antibody, and then activate the complement cascade.

273
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:24,000
Now that's called the classical pathway, and there's a famous mnemonic concerning this pathway that

274
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
GM makes classic cars.

275
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:37,000
So the company GM makes classic cars, and so IgG or IgM results in activation of the classical pathway.

276
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:44,000
The second pathway that's important, by which complement can be activated, is the alternative pathway.

277
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:50,000
The alternative pathway results in activation of complement by microbial products.

278
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:56,000
So the microbial products, they actually directly result in the activation of complement.

279
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:01,000
And so this is the alternative pathway, and that's pretty straightforward to understand.

280
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:07,000
The third pathway is the mannose binding lectin pathway, also known as the MBL pathway.

281
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:15,000
Now the mannose binding lectin pathway gets activated when MBL binds mannose on microorganisms,

282
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:23,000
and once the mannose is bound by MBL, that then allows for the activation of complement.

283
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:28,000
And so this is the third way by which complement can be activated.

284
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:35,000
The result of activation of complement is a little bit complicated, but can be summarized in just a very few simple principles.

285
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:44,000
All pathways sort of lead to the same result, which is that eventually C3 convertase gets generated.

286
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:51,000
When C3 convertase gets generated, C3 convertase converts C3 to C3A and C3B,

287
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:56,000
and then C3B joins in to help to produce C5 convertase,

288
00:23:56,000 --> 00:24:01,000
and C5 convertase converts C5 to C5A and C5B.

289
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:08,000
And then C5B actually complexes with C6 to C9 to produce something called the membrane attack complex.

290
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:14,000
And the membrane attack complex produces a hole within the membrane that allows for lysis of the microorganism.

291
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:17,000
So these are really the three key results.

292
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:25,000
C3 convertase generating C3A and C3B, C5 convertase generating C5A and C5B,

293
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:32,000
and then C5B joining with C6 through C9 to form the membrane attack complex.

294
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:38,000
So the key products that are generated by the complement system include C3A and C5A,

295
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
and we know what they do.

296
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
They trigger mast cell degranulation.

297
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:47,000
Can you remember, just from a couple slides back, what are the key activators of mast cells?

298
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:50,000
Number one, tissue trauma.

299
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:53,000
Number two, C3A and C5A.

300
00:24:53,000 --> 00:25:00,000
And number three, cross-linking of surface IgE by antigen.

301
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:01,000
Excellent.

302
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:04,000
So again, we want to burn these three key things in the back of our mind.

303
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
These are the three ways by which mast cells get activated.

304
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:12,000
The immediate response of the mast cell would be dumping of preformed histamine granules.

305
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:17,000
The histamines mediate vasodilation and increase vascular permeability.

306
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:20,000
And do you remember what the delayed response would be or the late response?

307
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:21,000
Excellent.

308
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:25,000
Production of arachidonic acid metabolites, in particular, leukotriene.

309
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:29,000
And again, all that's sort of the high-yield summary concerning mast cells.

310
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:36,000
The next key, one of the other key products of the complement system is C5A.

311
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:42,000
C5A is important because it is a chemotactic for neutrophils.

312
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:46,000
It's sort of one of the things that helps to activate and bring in the neutrophils.

313
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:53,000
So I can stop here and say, do you remember the four key products that result in the activation and bringing in of neutrophils?

314
00:25:53,000 --> 00:26:00,000
Leukotriene B4, C5A, IL8, and bacterial products.

315
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:01,000
Excellent.

316
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:03,000
So those are the four key things that bring in the neutrophils.

317
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:08,000
Now, another product is C3B, which is an opsonin for phagocytosis.

318
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:09,000
What's an opsonin?

319
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:16,000
Basically, remember that when the neutrophils get into tissue, the way by which they destroy things is going to be phagocytosis.

320
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:21,000
They go in there, they consume things, and then they destroy that which they consume.

321
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:25,000
So that's called phagocytosis, to eat things from the outside world.

322
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:33,000
Now, phagocytosis is usually blind, i.e., the neutrophil just sort of goes in and grabs things randomly and begins to consume it once it's activated.

323
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:39,000
However, one of the ways by which that phagocytosis can be assisted is by opsonins.

324
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:48,000
And opsonins are proteins that the neutrophil can recognize, which then tell it to consume that which is attached to that protein.

325
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
And so one important opsonin is C3B.

326
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:55,000
And we'll talk a little bit more about opsonins a little later in our discussion.

327
00:26:55,000 --> 00:27:00,000
And the final key product of the complement system is the membrane attack complex.

328
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:09,000
Basically, if you have a microbe and complement gets activated on the microbe, membrane attack complex is produced and that creates a hole in the cell membrane,

329
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:16,000
which then dysregulates the relationship of that cell to the outside world and then results in death of that cell.

330
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:31,000
So the membrane attack complex, again, is formed by the C5B, which then goes off and joins with C6 to C9 to then produce the membrane attack complex.

331
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:37,000
So these are some of the key products and, again, relatively high yield for examinations.

332
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:43,000
The next of the acute inflammatory mediators is Hagemann factor.

333
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:47,000
Hagemann factor is an inactive pro-inflammatory protein that's produced in the liver.

334
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:53,000
And it gets activated upon exposure to subendothelial or tissue collagen.

335
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:58,000
So this is one of the important factors that helps to activate acute inflammation.

336
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:04,000
And I would say if one important thing for examinations that you want to remember about Hagemann factor,

337
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:10,000
I would say that Hagemann factor plays an important role in DIC, especially in severe gram-negative sepsis.

338
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:17,000
So when there's severe gram-negative sepsis, the gram-negative organisms also have the ability to activate Hagemann factor,

339
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:21,000
and that activation of Hagemann factor plays an important role in DIC.

340
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:28,000
And the reason it plays an important role in DIC is because Hagemann factor, in turn, it activates the following systems.

341
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:34,000
It activates coagulation and the fibrinolytic system, allowing for the development of DIC.

342
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:37,000
It also activates complement and the kinin system.

343
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:43,000
But this activation of the coagulation and fibrinolytic systems is relatively high yield.

344
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:49,000
Again, you want to tie that into sort of one mechanism by which DIC can occur.

345
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:55,000
And again, the other things that Hagemann factor does, it activates complement, which helps to mediate acute inflammation,

346
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:58,000
and then activates something called the kinin system.

347
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:00,000
So what is the kinin system?

348
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:05,000
The kinin system cleaves high molecular weight kininogen to bradykinin.

349
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:12,000
And bradykinin mediates vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, and pain.

350
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:20,000
The way I remember bradykinin is that it sort of does the same thing as histamine, i.e. vasodilation and increased vascular permeability,

351
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:23,000
but in addition, it mediates pain.

352
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:28,000
And we could stop here and remind ourselves that what are the two things that mediate pain?

353
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:36,000
Prostaglandin E2, remember prostaglandin E2 mediates fever and pain.

354
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,000
And the second of them is bradykinin.

355
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:43,000
So pain, which is normally experienced with an acute inflammatory response,

356
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:48,000
is actually mediated by two things, bradykinin and prostaglandin E2.

357
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:53,000
I'm just trying to sort of hammer home some of the high yields so that you take them away from this lecture.

358
00:29:53,000 --> 00:30:01,000
Once acute inflammation is activated, all of these mediators are sort of played together as a symphony.

359
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:06,000
And that symphony is going to result in the cardinal signs of inflammation.

360
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:11,000
So now we're sort of putting together all the mediators and saying, so what is the overall result?

361
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:15,000
And the overall result are the cardinal signs of acute inflammation.

362
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:17,000
So what are these signs?

363
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:19,000
These we're very familiar with.

364
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:23,000
The rubor or redness and warmth, that's called calor.

365
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:26,000
This is the first of the two key cardinal signs.

366
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:32,000
It's not sufficient for examination purposes to just know the cardinal signs,

367
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:35,000
but instead you have to know what mediates them and why they occur.

368
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:37,000
So let's just think for a second.

369
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:39,000
If we're going to get redness in a tissue,

370
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:42,000
the way by which we're going to get redness is by increasing the amount of blood,

371
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:45,000
because that's what's going to give the red color to the tissue.

372
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:49,000
And so redness actually occurs due to vasodilation.

373
00:30:49,000 --> 00:30:53,000
And vasodilation will be mediated at the level of the arteriole.

374
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:57,000
Once vasodilation occurs, increased blood will go into the tissue.

375
00:30:57,000 --> 00:31:02,000
And when there's increased blood within the tissue, that will provide the redness that we see in acute inflammation.

376
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:06,000
So it's mediated by vasodilation.

377
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:09,000
It occurs at the arteriolar, smooth muscle.

378
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:11,000
And the key mediators are histamine.

379
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:14,000
Remember that histamine mediates vasodilation.

380
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:19,000
Prostaglandins, in particular prostaglandin I2, D2, and E2.

381
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:21,000
And bradyklinin.

382
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:24,000
These are all key mediators of vasodilation.

383
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:27,000
And the most important of these is actually histamine.

384
00:31:27,000 --> 00:31:32,000
Histamine is the primary molecule that mediates this response.

385
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:34,000
So that's the way by which we get redness.

386
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:36,000
Now in the same way, we get warmth.

387
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:37,000
It's the exact same mechanism.

388
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:39,000
Because what is warmth?

389
00:31:39,000 --> 00:31:46,000
Warmth is basically an increased amount of warm blood within that particular area of tissue.

390
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:47,000
That's what makes it warm.

391
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:50,000
There's an increased amount of blood, and the blood is actually warm.

392
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:52,000
So why would we get increased, why would we get the warmth?

393
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:53,000
How would that occur?

394
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:59,000
That would occur by vasodilation at the level of the arteriolar, smooth muscle.

395
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:03,000
And the key mediators are again going to be histamine, which is number one.

396
00:32:03,000 --> 00:32:10,000
And then prostaglandins, in particular prostaglandin I2, D2, and E2.

397
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:12,000
And bradykinin.

398
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:19,000
So again, not hard, but important to understand the basis of what we're going to see clinically.

399
00:32:19,000 --> 00:32:25,000
The next of the key features that we see in acute inflammation clinically is swelling, also called tumor.

400
00:32:25,000 --> 00:32:27,000
And swelling, how is that going to occur?

401
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:34,000
It occurs by leakage of fluid from the post-capillary venule into the interstitial space.

402
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:41,000
Again, I've already kind of explained this, but it's important to know that that leakage occurs at the post-capillary venule.

403
00:32:41,000 --> 00:32:45,000
And the key mediators are going to be histamine.

404
00:32:45,000 --> 00:32:47,000
Histamine is sort of the number one mediator.

405
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:50,000
And another important mediator is going to be tissue damage.

406
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:56,000
And the idea here is that if you've got a blood vessel and it's lined by endothelial cells,

407
00:32:56,000 --> 00:33:01,000
normally fluid cannot leave the blood vessel space.

408
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:12,000
However, if you damage the endothelium so that there is a disruption of the wall of the blood vessel due to tissue damage, fluid can easily leak out.

409
00:33:12,000 --> 00:33:15,000
So that's what's going to result in the swelling.

410
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:23,000
Number one, histamine, which actually helps to separate the endothelial cells, and tissue damage, which actually disrupts the endothelial cells.

411
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:25,000
So that's going to result in swelling.

412
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:28,000
Pain is another cardinal sign of acute inflammation.

413
00:33:28,000 --> 00:33:32,000
When a patient has acute inflammation, the reason is often painful.

414
00:33:32,000 --> 00:33:35,000
And there's two things that mediate pain, and I've already covered them.

415
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:42,000
Bradykinin, which was generated from high molecular weight kininogen.

416
00:33:42,000 --> 00:33:48,000
And then number two, prostaglandin E2, which remember prostaglandin E2 mediates what?

417
00:33:48,000 --> 00:33:50,000
Fever and pain.

418
00:33:50,000 --> 00:33:51,000
Very good.

419
00:33:51,000 --> 00:34:03,000
So prostaglandin E2 and bradykinin, the mechanism by which they allow for the formation of pain or the feeling of pain is that they sensitize the sensory nerve endings.

420
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:07,000
They sensitize the sensory nerve endings.

421
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:18,280
And the next sign of, or I would say the final sign of acute inflammation, at least the final cardinal sign of acute inflammation, is fever.

422
00:34:18,280 --> 00:34:23,280
And fever, the mechanism is very important to know.

423
00:34:23,280 --> 00:34:25,280
Examiners love to go after this.

424
00:34:25,280 --> 00:34:29,280
So the way by which fever occurs is that the macrophages, and that's important.

425
00:34:29,280 --> 00:34:32,280
The macrophages release IL-1 and TNF.

426
00:34:32,280 --> 00:34:36,280
And IL-1 and TNF basically get into the bloodstream.

427
00:34:36,280 --> 00:34:38,280
They go through the blood.

428
00:34:38,280 --> 00:34:47,280
They then hit the perivascular cells of the hypothalamus, so that the temperature regulation of the body is mediated in the hypothalamus.

429
00:34:47,280 --> 00:34:51,280
And in those cells, they increase COX activity.

430
00:34:51,280 --> 00:35:00,280
The COX or cyclooxygenase activity increases prostaglandin E2, which then, remember, leads to fever, as I've said many times before.

431
00:35:00,280 --> 00:35:04,280
Prostaglandin E2 raises the temperature set point in the hypothalamus.

432
00:35:04,280 --> 00:35:07,280
So let's review that one more time because it's super high yield.

433
00:35:07,280 --> 00:35:10,280
Macrophages produce IL-1 and TNF.

434
00:35:10,280 --> 00:35:14,280
The IL-1 and TNF gets into the perivascular cells of the hypothalamus.

435
00:35:14,280 --> 00:35:21,280
It increases COX activity, and the increased COX activity is going to result in the production of prostaglandin E2.

436
